1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,600
Section 3

2
00:00:01,600 --> 00:00:02,405
TRUDIE:

3
00:00:02,405 --> 00:00:03,089
OK, Stewart.

4
00:00:03,089 --> 00:00:05,877
We need to start planning our paper on public libraries.

5
00:00:05,877 --> 00:00:08,180
Have you thought of an angle yet?

6
00:00:08,180 --> 00:00:08,563
STEWART:

7
00:00:08,563 --> 00:00:11,631
Well, there's so much we could look into.

8
00:00:11,631 --> 00:00:15,082
How libraries have changed over the centuries, for instance,

9
00:00:15,082 --> 00:00:17,383
or how different countries organise them.

10
00:00:17,383 --> 00:00:19,300
What do you think, Trudie?

11
00:00:19,300 --> 00:00:19,636
TRUDIE:

12
00:00:19,636 --> 00:00:21,985
Maybe we should concentrate on this country,

13
00:00:21,985 --> 00:00:26,683
and try and relate the changes in libraries to external developments, like the fact

14
00:00:26,683 --> 00:00:30,039
that far more people can read than a century ago,

15
00:00:30,039 --> 00:00:33,730
and that the local population may speak lots of different languages.

16
00:00:33,730 --> 00:00:34,062
STEWART:

17
00:00:34,062 --> 00:00:38,040
We could include something about changes in the source of funding, too.

18
00:00:38,040 --> 00:00:38,349
TRUDIE:

19
00:00:38,349 --> 00:00:38,658
Yes,

20
00:00:38,658 --> 00:00:41,746
but remember we're only supposed to write a short paper,

21
00:00:41,746 --> 00:00:45,760
so it's probably best if we don't go into funding in any detail.

22
00:00:45,760 --> 00:00:46,221
STEWART:

23
00:00:46,221 --> 00:00:46,682
Right.

24
00:00:46,682 --> 00:00:51,750
Well, shall we just brainstorm a few ideas, to get started?

25
00:00:51,750 --> 00:00:52,085
TRUDIE:

26
00:00:52,085 --> 00:00:52,421
OK.

27
00:00:52,421 --> 00:00:57,115
We obviously need to look at the impact of new technology, particularly the internet.

28
00:00:57,115 --> 00:01:01,809
Now that lots of books have been digitalised, people can access them from their

29
00:01:01,809 --> 00:01:03,150
own computers at home.

30
00:01:03,150 --> 00:01:03,518
STEWART:

31
00:01:03,518 --> 00:01:06,830
And if everyone did that, libraries would be obsolete.

32
00:01:06,830 --> 00:01:07,630
TRUDIE:

33
00:01:07,630 --> 00:01:08,430
Yes.

34
00:01:08,430 --> 00:01:08,840
STEWART:

35
00:01:08,840 --> 00:01:14,584
But the digitalised books that are available online for free are mostly out of

36
00:01:14,584 --> 00:01:15,814
copyright, aren't they?

37
00:01:15,814 --> 00:01:21,148
And copyright in this country lasts for 70 years after the author dies.

38
00:01:21,148 --> 00:01:25,250
So you won't find the latest best-seller or up-to-date information.

39
00:01:25,250 --> 00:01:25,568
TRUDIE:

40
00:01:25,568 --> 00:01:26,842
That's an important point.

41
00:01:26,842 --> 00:01:31,299
Anyway, I find it hard to concentrate when I'm reading a long text on

42
00:01:31,299 --> 00:01:31,936
a screen.

43
00:01:31,936 --> 00:01:34,165
I'd much rather read a physical book.

44
00:01:34,165 --> 00:01:37,030
And it takes longer to read on a screen.

45
00:01:37,030 --> 00:01:37,460
STEWART:

46
00:01:37,460 --> 00:01:39,180
Oh, I prefer it.

47
00:01:39,180 --> 00:01:42,190
I suppose it's just a personal preference.

48
00:01:42,190 --> 00:01:42,539
TRUDIE:

49
00:01:42,539 --> 00:01:42,888
Mm.

50
00:01:42,888 --> 00:01:47,428
I expect that libraries will go on evolving in the next few years.

51
00:01:47,428 --> 00:01:52,316
Some have already become centres where community activities take place, like local clubs meeting

52
00:01:52,316 --> 00:01:52,666
there.

53
00:01:52,666 --> 00:01:55,110
I think that'll become even more common.

54
00:01:55,110 --> 00:01:55,442
STEWART:

55
00:01:55,442 --> 00:01:57,103
I'd like to think so,

56
00:01:57,103 --> 00:02:00,094
and that they'll still be serving their traditional function,

57
00:02:00,094 --> 00:02:01,755
but I'm not so sure.

58
00:02:01,755 --> 00:02:03,748
There are financial implications, after all.

59
00:02:03,748 --> 00:02:08,067
What I'm afraid will happen is that books and magazines will all disappear,

60
00:02:08,067 --> 00:02:11,058
and there'll just be rows and rows of computers.

61
00:02:11,058 --> 00:02:14,380
They won't look anything like the libraries we're used to.

62
00:02:14,380 --> 00:02:14,900
TRUDIE:

63
00:02:14,900 --> 00:02:16,460
Well, we'll see.

64
00:02:16,460 --> 00:02:16,781
TRUDIE:

65
00:02:16,781 --> 00:02:18,385
I've just had an idea.

66
00:02:18,385 --> 00:02:22,877
Why don't we make an in-depth study of our local public library as background

67
00:02:22,877 --> 00:02:23,840
to our paper?

68
00:02:23,840 --> 00:02:24,171
STEWART:

69
00:02:24,171 --> 00:02:24,502
Yes,

70
00:02:24,502 --> 00:02:25,494
that'd be interesting,

71
00:02:25,494 --> 00:02:27,480
and raise all sorts of issues.

72
00:02:27,480 --> 00:02:32,112
Let's make a list of possible things we could ask about, then work out

73
00:02:32,112 --> 00:02:33,435
some sort of structure.

74
00:02:33,435 --> 00:02:36,744
For instance, um, we could interview some of the staff,

75
00:02:36,744 --> 00:02:40,053
and find out whether the library has its own budget,

76
00:02:40,053 --> 00:02:42,700
or if that's controlled by the local council.

77
00:02:42,700 --> 00:02:42,983
TRUDIE:

78
00:02:42,983 --> 00:02:44,395
And what their policies are.

79
00:02:44,395 --> 00:02:46,091
I know they don't allow food,

80
00:02:46,091 --> 00:02:49,764
but I'd love to find out what types of noise they ban - there

81
00:02:49,764 --> 00:02:52,024
always seems to be a lot of talking,

82
00:02:52,024 --> 00:02:52,872
but never music.

83
00:02:52,872 --> 00:02:55,980
I don't know if that's a policy or it just happens.

84
00:02:55,980 --> 00:02:56,386
STEWART:

85
00:02:56,386 --> 00:02:58,008
Ah, I've often wondered.

86
00:02:58,008 --> 00:03:03,281
Then there are things like how the library is affected by employment laws.

87
00:03:03,281 --> 00:03:07,743
I suppose there are rules about working hours, facilities for staff,

88
00:03:07,743 --> 00:03:08,960
and so on.

89
00:03:08,960 --> 00:03:09,298
TRUDIE:

90
00:03:09,298 --> 00:03:09,636
Right.

91
00:03:09,636 --> 00:03:13,689
Then there are other issues relating to the design of the building

92
00:03:13,689 --> 00:03:15,378
and how customers use it.

93
00:03:15,378 --> 00:03:19,094
Like what measures does the library take to ensure their safety?

94
00:03:19,094 --> 00:03:21,459
They'd need floor coverings that aren't slippery,

95
00:03:21,459 --> 00:03:23,148
and emergency exits, for instance.

96
00:03:23,148 --> 00:03:23,486
Oh,

97
00:03:23,486 --> 00:03:27,878
and another thing - there's the question of the kind of insurance the library

98
00:03:27,878 --> 00:03:30,580
needs to have, in case anyone gets injured.

99
00:03:30,580 --> 00:03:30,944
STEWART:

100
00:03:30,944 --> 00:03:31,309
Yes,

101
00:03:31,309 --> 00:03:33,494
that's something else to find out.

102
00:03:33,494 --> 00:03:35,680
You know something I've often wondered?

103
00:03:35,680 --> 00:03:36,203
TRUDIE:

104
00:03:36,203 --> 00:03:37,250
What's that?

105
00:03:37,250 --> 00:03:37,584
STEWART:

106
00:03:37,584 --> 00:03:41,928
Well, you know they've got an archive of local newspapers going back years?

107
00:03:41,928 --> 00:03:46,606
Well, next to it they've got the diary of a well-known politician from the

108
00:03:46,606 --> 00:03:47,608
late 19th century.

109
00:03:47,608 --> 00:03:49,279
I wonder why it's there.

110
00:03:49,279 --> 00:03:52,620
Do you know what his connection was with this area?

111
00:03:52,620 --> 00:03:52,963
TRUDIE:

112
00:03:52,963 --> 00:03:53,648
No idea.

113
00:03:53,648 --> 00:03:57,420
Let's add it to our list of things to find out.

114
00:03:57,420 --> 00:04:01,876
Oh, I've just thought - you know people might ask in the library about

115
00:04:01,876 --> 00:04:03,590
local organisations, like sports clubs?

116
00:04:03,590 --> 00:04:03,933
Well.

117
00:04:03,933 --> 00:04:06,333
I wonder if they keep a database,

118
00:04:06,333 --> 00:04:08,390
or whether they just look online.

119
00:04:08,390 --> 00:04:08,714
STEWART:

120
00:04:08,714 --> 00:04:09,037
Right.

121
00:04:09,037 --> 00:04:14,539
I quite fancy finding out what the differences are between a library that's open to the public

122
00:04:14,539 --> 00:04:19,070
and one that's part of a museum, for example - they must be very different.

123
00:04:19,070 --> 00:04:19,371
TRUDIE:

124
00:04:19,371 --> 00:04:19,672
Mmm.

125
00:04:19,672 --> 00:04:22,080
Then something else I'd like to know is ...
